"""New content added in response to local-reviewer comments on the BMC Bioinformatics submission:
(1) a Results subsection comparing classification performance AND explainability evaluation against
recent literature (with verified, cited numbers and explicit split-protocol caveats), and (2) two
Discussion subsections that interpret the present results against that literature and state the
practical significance, generalizability and limits of the evaluation framework itself. Both are
appended after the existing numbered sequences (Results 4.10 after 4.9; Discussion 5.8-5.9 after 5.7),
so no existing table, figure or section number is renumbered."""
import json
import numpy as np, pandas as pd
from common import *
from ms_results_cls import pm, fp
import ms_results_xai as RX

RES = OUT / 'results'
S = json.load(open(RES / 'cls_summary.json')); agg = S['agg']; K = len(S['seeds'])
XA = RX.XA; PA = RX.PA; ac = RX.ac; REF = 42; R = XA[REF]


def sec_null_faith(M):
    nf = pd.read_csv(RES / 'null_faith_per_seed.csv'); nfs = json.load(open(RES / 'null_faith_summary.json'))
    Kx = len(nf)
    M.h(2, '4.11 Null-controlled faithfulness: excess insertion and deletion gain')
    M.p(f"The deletion/insertion controls of Section 4.6 use a uniformly random patch order, which shows only that a structured revealing order beats no structure, not that an image's own Grad-CAM++ map beats a plausible but wrong explanation of similar visual character. The excess insertion gain (EIG) and excess deletion gain (EDG) of Section 3.9 apply the excess-over-null logic of Section 3.8 to faithfulness, with the maps of three other random test images as cross-image null order (mean-image baseline, all five seeds).")
    M.p(f"Both quantities were positive in every seed: median EIG ranged from {nfs['EIG']['median_range'][0]:.4f} to {nfs['EIG']['median_range'][1]:.4f} across seeds (mean of seed medians {nfs['EIG']['median_mean']:.4f} ± {nfs['EIG']['median_sd']:.4f}; positive for {100 * nfs['EIG']['frac_pos_range'][0]:.0f}–{100 * nfs['EIG']['frac_pos_range'][1]:.0f}% of images; Wilcoxon p < 0.05 in {nfs['EIG']['n_seeds_wilcoxon_sig']}/{nfs['EIG']['n_seeds']} seeds), and median EDG ranged from {nfs['EDG']['median_range'][0]:.4f} to {nfs['EDG']['median_range'][1]:.4f} (mean of seed medians {nfs['EDG']['median_mean']:.4f} ± {nfs['EDG']['median_sd']:.4f}; positive for {100 * nfs['EDG']['frac_pos_range'][0]:.0f}–{100 * nfs['EDG']['frac_pos_range'][1]:.0f}% of images; Wilcoxon p < 0.05 in {nfs['EDG']['n_seeds_wilcoxon_sig']}/{nfs['EDG']['n_seeds']} seeds). This differs from the raw deletion gain of Section 4.6, which was negative in most seeds: against a uniformly random order the map often did not look more informative on deletion, but against a plausible explanation from a different lesion it was more informative on both insertion and deletion in every seed. Excess insertion and deletion gain were positively correlated with excess LSAS in most seeds (EIG: ρ range {nfs['EIG']['rho_eshuf_range'][0]:+.2f} to {nfs['EIG']['rho_eshuf_range'][1]:+.2f}, significant in {nfs['EIG']['n_seeds_rho_eshuf_sig']}/{Kx} seeds; EDG: ρ range {nfs['EDG']['rho_eshuf_range'][0]:+.2f} to {nfs['EDG']['rho_eshuf_range'][1]:+.2f}, significant in {nfs['EDG']['n_seeds_rho_eshuf_sig']}/{Kx} seeds), and only weakly related to lesion area (EIG: ρ range {nfs['EIG']['rho_area_range'][0]:+.2f} to {nfs['EIG']['rho_area_range'][1]:+.2f}; EDG: ρ range {nfs['EDG']['rho_area_range'][0]:+.2f} to {nfs['EDG']['rho_area_range'][1]:+.2f}), indicating that the null-controlled faithfulness gain, unlike raw LSAS, is not primarily a geometric artefact of lesion size.")
    rows = []
    for _, r in nf.iterrows():
        rows.append([f"Seed {int(r.seed)}", f"{r.EIG_median:.4f} ({r.EIG_q1:.4f}–{r.EIG_q3:.4f})", f"{100 * r.EIG_frac_pos:.0f}%", f"{r.EIG_wilcoxon_p:.2e}", f"{r.EIG_rho_eshuf:+.2f}",
                     f"{r.EDG_median:.4f} ({r.EDG_q1:.4f}–{r.EDG_q3:.4f})", f"{100 * r.EDG_frac_pos:.0f}%", f"{r.EDG_wilcoxon_p:.2e}", f"{r.EDG_rho_eshuf:+.2f}"])
    M.table(['Run', 'EIG, median (IQR)', 'EIG > 0', 'EIG Wilcoxon p', 'EIG ρ with excess LSAS', 'EDG, median (IQR)', 'EDG > 0', 'EDG Wilcoxon p', 'EDG ρ with excess LSAS'], rows,
            'Null-controlled excess insertion gain (EIG) and excess deletion gain (EDG) for every seed, all 990 test images.',
            'EIG = IG(observed Grad-CAM++ order) − IG(cross-image null order); EDG = DG(cross-image null order) − DG(observed order); positive values indicate the image’s own explanation is more functionally informative than a plausible but mismatched explanation. Cross-image null order uses the Grad-CAM++ maps of three randomly chosen other test images per target image (mean-image baseline).')
    ins_g = nf.ins_cam_med.mean() - nf.ins_nullcam_med.mean(); ins_g2 = nf.ins_nullcam_med.mean() - nf.ins_rand_med.mean()
    M.p(f"As a consistency check, the cross-image null insertion curve sat between the model's own curve and a uniformly random curve in every seed (mean median insertion AUC: observed {nf.ins_cam_med.mean():.3f}, cross-image null {nf.ins_nullcam_med.mean():.3f}, random {nf.ins_rand_med.mean():.3f}), as expected for a spatially coherent but mismatched map.")
    sens = pd.read_csv(RES / 'null_faith_sensitivity.csv')
    M.p("__Sensitivity of EIG/EDG to the null definition.__ The three-donor, mean-baseline, random-selection null above is one point in a space of possible cross-image nulls, and another choice could in principle change the sign or support of EIG/EDG. This was checked on the reference seed by varying the number of donors (1, 3, 5, 10, 20; random selection, mean baseline), the baseline (blurred image, 3 donors) and the donor-selection rule (3 donors chosen at random, from images with the same predicted class, from images with a different predicted class, or as the three images with the closest lesion-mask area; mean baseline).")
    rows = []
    for _, r in sens.iterrows():
        rows.append([f"{int(r.donor_k)}", r.baseline, r.selection.replace('_', ' '),
                     f"{r.EIG_median:+.4f} ({r.EIG_q1:+.4f}–{r.EIG_q3:+.4f})", f"{100 * r.EIG_frac_pos:.0f}%", f"{r.EIG_wilcoxon_p:.1e}",
                     f"{r.EDG_median:+.4f} ({r.EDG_q1:+.4f}–{r.EDG_q3:+.4f})", f"{100 * r.EDG_frac_pos:.0f}%", f"{r.EDG_wilcoxon_p:.1e}"])
    M.table(['Donors', 'Baseline', 'Donor selection', 'EIG, median (IQR)', 'EIG > 0', 'EIG Wilcoxon p', 'EDG, median (IQR)', 'EDG > 0', 'EDG Wilcoxon p'], rows,
            'Sensitivity of excess insertion/deletion gain to donor count, baseline and donor-selection rule (reference seed, all 990 test images).',
            'Rows 1-5 vary donor count (mean baseline, random selection; row 2, 3 donors, is the configuration of [[T:null_faith]] redrawn with an independent seed). Row 6 uses the blurred-image baseline. Rows 7-9 use 3 donors and the mean baseline with donors from the same predicted class, a different predicted class, and the closest lesion-mask area, respectively.')
    n_cfg = len(sens); n_pos_eig = int((sens.EIG_median > 0).sum()); n_pos_edg = int((sens.EDG_median > 0).sum())
    n_sig_eig = int((sens.EIG_wilcoxon_p < 0.05).sum()); n_sig_edg = int((sens.EDG_wilcoxon_p < 0.05).sum())
    med_eig = sens.EIG_median.values; med_edg = sens.EDG_median.values
    dcount = sens[sens.baseline.eq('mean') & sens.selection.eq('random')].sort_values('donor_k')
    dtrend_eig = 'fell' if dcount.EIG_median.is_monotonic_decreasing else ('rose' if dcount.EIG_median.is_monotonic_increasing else 'was non-monotonic')
    same_row = sens[sens.selection == 'same_class'].iloc[0]; diff_row = sens[sens.selection == 'diff_class'].iloc[0]; area_row = sens[sens.selection == 'area_matched'].iloc[0]
    blur_row = sens[sens.baseline == 'blur'].iloc[0]
    M.p(f"Median EIG was positive in {n_pos_eig}/{n_cfg} configurations tested ({med_eig.min():+.4f} to {med_eig.max():+.4f} across all rows of [[T:sens]], Wilcoxon-significant in {n_sig_eig}/{n_cfg}), and median EDG was positive in {n_pos_edg}/{n_cfg} (Wilcoxon-significant in {n_sig_edg}/{n_cfg}). Across the donor-count sweep (1, 3, 5, 10, 20 donors, mean baseline, random selection), median EIG {dtrend_eig} with donor count (from {dcount.EIG_median.iloc[0]:+.4f} at 1 donor to {dcount.EIG_median.iloc[-1]:+.4f} at 20); a larger donor pool gives a smoother, more generic null order, which changes the gap's magnitude without necessarily changing its sign. Swapping the mean-image baseline for the blurred-image baseline (3 donors) gave median EIG {blur_row.EIG_median:+.4f} and EDG {blur_row.EDG_median:+.4f}, both in the same direction as the mean-baseline result. Donor selection probes different confounds: same-predicted-class donors are the strictest test, since they share the model's class-typical spatial pattern (median EIG {same_row.EIG_median:+.4f}, EDG {same_row.EDG_median:+.4f}); different-predicted-class donors are the least strict (EIG {diff_row.EIG_median:+.4f}, EDG {diff_row.EDG_median:+.4f}); and area-matched donors rule out lesion size (EIG {area_row.EIG_median:+.4f}, EDG {area_row.EDG_median:+.4f}). The check used the reference seed and a single donor draw per configuration, so it constrains rather than replaces the five-seed headline result of [[T:null_faith]].")


def sec_sota(M):
    M.h(2, '4.10 Comparison with recent literature on skin-lesion classification and explainability evaluation')
    M.p(f"[[T:sota]] places the present five-seed result (accuracy {pm(agg['acc']['mean'], agg['acc']['sd'])}, macro-F1 {pm(agg['f1']['mean'], agg['f1']['sd'])}, macro-AUROC {pm(agg['auroc']['mean'], agg['auroc']['sd'])}) alongside recent HAM10000/ISIC classification studies and studies that, like this one, combine classification with an explainability evaluation. The comparison is not like-for-like: architectures, class sets, augmentation and, above all, train/test partitioning differ, and only some studies report whether their split was at the lesion or image level; where a protocol could not be determined from the published description, this is stated rather than assumed.")
    rows = [
        ['This study', '2026', 'HAM10000 (7-class)', 'DenseNet121', 'Lesion-level, 5-seed, natural class prevalence', f"{agg['acc']['mean']:.3f} ± {agg['acc']['sd']:.3f}", f"{agg['f1']['mean']:.3f} ± {agg['f1']['sd']:.3f}", f"{agg['auroc']['mean']:.3f}", 'Yes (5)', 'Yes, decomposed (PAD-UFES-20)', 'Null-controlled localization, perturbation faithfulness, stability, skin-tone proxy'],
        ['Jamaludin & Kim, 2026 [@jamaludin2026]', '2026', 'HAM10000 (7-class)', 'ResNet50 (+focal loss, TTA)', 'Verified lesion-level, 5-seed', '0.784 ± 0.007', '0.804', 'n/r', 'Yes (5)', 'No', 'No'],
        [' same pipeline, leakage-prone split [@jamaludin2026]', '2026', 'HAM10000 (7-class)', 'ResNet50 (identical)', 'Image-level (22.8% lesion overlap)', '0.946', 'n/r', 'n/r', 'n/r', 'No', 'No'],
        ['Combalia et al., 2022 [@combalia2022]', '2022', 'HAM10000 vs. BCN20000 (8-class)', '129 algorithms, 64 teams (ISIC 2019 Challenge)', 'Fixed challenge test sets; best submission', '82.0% BAcc (HAM10000) / 58.8% BAcc (BCN20000, external)', 'n/r', 'n/r', 'Multi-team, not multi-seed', 'Yes (cross-archive, 4 countries)', 'No'],
        ['Dağoğlu Hark, 2025 [@dagoglu2025]', '2025', 'HAM10000 (7-class)', 'EfficientNetB0 / MobileNetV2', '3-fold × 3-seed CV, natural prevalence', '0.715–0.725 (±0.03–0.05)', '0.46–0.53 (±0.06–0.16)', 'n/r', 'Yes (3×3)', 'No', 'No'],
        ['Khan et al. (SynthraXCoreNet), 2026 [@khan2026]', '2026', 'HAM10000 + 3 other datasets (per-dataset training)', 'Six-CNN soft-voting ensemble', 'Not stated', '98.85% (95% CI half-width 0.68)', '0.99', 'n/r', 'No (ensemble of architectures, not seeds)', 'Per-dataset only, not cross-dataset transfer', 'Composite faithfulness/robustness/complexity/axiomatic scores (no geometric null control)'],
        ['Murali & Mazumder (DermaScanAI), 2026 [@murali2026]', '2026', 'HAM10000 (7-class)', 'Multi-scale CNN + lightweight transformer + SE blocks', 'Not stated', '94.8%', '91.9%', '0.957', 'No', 'No', 'Grad-CAM (qualitative only)'],
        ['Sonune et al., 2026 [@sonune2026]', '2026', 'HAM10000 (7-class)', 'Six-CNN soft-voting ensemble', 'Not stated', '89.37%', 'n/r (macro-AUROC 0.985)', '0.985', 'No', 'No', 'Grad-CAM, LIME, occlusion (qualitative spatial coherence)'],
        ['Fernandez et al., 2025 [@fernandez2025]', '2025', 'ISIC archive, 9-class (not HAM10000)', 'Swin-Tiny (best of 5 architectures)', 'Not stated; different dataset/task', '78.2%', '71.2%', 'n/r', 'Not stated', 'No', 'Morphometric (entropy, symmetry) correct-vs-incorrect saliency comparison'],
        ['Sekaran et al. (SCES-QNet), 2026 [@sekaran2026]', '2026', 'HAM10000 → ISIC2019 / PAD-UFES-20 (external)', 'Distributed hybrid quantum network', '5 independent runs; source-only transfer', '98.6% BAcc (HAM10000)', '98.4% (HAM10000) / 94.8% (PAD-UFES-20, external)', '0.998 (HAM10000) / 0.989 (PAD-UFES-20)', 'Yes (5)', 'Yes, near-ceiling retention claimed', 'Explanation-stability score (ESS); no faithfulness or geometric null control reported'],
        ['Cino et al., 2025 [@cino2025]', '2025', 'ISIC 2019 (not HAM10000)', 'Six CNNs + test-time augmentation', 'Not stated', '97.58% BAcc (with TTA)', 'n/r', 'n/r', 'Not stated', 'No', 'Grad-CAM, t-SNE (qualitative only)'],
    ]
    M.table(['Study', 'Year', 'Dataset(s)', 'Architecture', 'Split protocol', 'Accuracy / balanced accuracy', 'Macro-F1', 'AUROC', 'Multi-seed', 'External validation', 'Explainability evaluation'], rows,
            'Recent skin-lesion classification and explainability-evaluation studies, compared with the present five-seed result.',
            'BAcc = balanced accuracy; n/r = not reported in the cited source. Split protocol and multi-seed status are transcribed from each study’s methods section where stated, and marked “not stated” otherwise, which describes the study’s reporting, not what was done. The Jamaludin row pair is one architecture and code under two partitioning schemes.')
    M.p(f"Three patterns are visible in [[T:sota]]. First, the highest accuracies (94.8-98.85%, [@khan2026,murali2026]) and the highest HAM10000 balanced accuracy under a verified lesion-level protocol ({pm(agg['acc']['mean'], agg['acc']['sd'])} here; 78.4% ± 0.7% in [@jamaludin2026]) differ by roughly 20 percentage points, and the one study reporting the identical pipeline under both an image-level and a verified lesion-level split shows a 16.2-point drop (94.57% to 78.41%) from partitioning alone [@jamaludin2026]. None of the higher-accuracy studies states whether its split was at the lesion level, so leakage can be neither excluded nor confirmed. Second, macro-averaged performance is more sensitive to protocol and partitioning than accuracy: an independent 3-fold-by-3-seed study found macro-F1 for the same model varying between 0.27 and 0.53 while accuracy stayed within a narrow band [@dagoglu2025], consistent with the {pm(agg['f1']['mean'], agg['f1']['sd'])} macro-F1 obtained here alongside {pm(agg['acc']['mean'], agg['acc']['sd'])} accuracy. Third, explainability evaluation in this literature is predominantly qualitative (Grad-CAM overlays or a single composite score) rather than null-controlled: no compared study separates a mask-overlap statistic from the geometry of its reference region, and only one [@sekaran2026] evaluates cross-dataset explanation behaviour on the same dataset pair, without a faithfulness test comparable to Section 3.9. That study's 94.8% macro-F1 on PAD-UFES-20 after HAM10000-only training is far more optimistic than the replicated external-generalization losses reported for dermoscopy and medical imaging [@combalia2022,yu2022ext,fogelberg2023,matta2024] and than the residual drop found here (Section 4.9); as a single study without independent replication, it is noted as a claim, not a benchmark to be matched.")


def sec_discussion_extra(M):
    M.h(2, '5.8 Comparative interpretation of measured performance and external generalization')
    M.p(f"The accuracy and macro-F1 reported here ({pm(agg['acc']['mean'], agg['acc']['sd'])} and {pm(agg['f1']['mean'], agg['f1']['sd'])}) are lower than the 90-99% accuracies of several recent HAM10000 studies ([[T:sota]]) and might superficially suggest a weaker classifier. Three considerations argue against that reading. First, an independent 2026 audit found that correcting an image-level (leakage-prone) split to a verified lesion-level split with five-seed evaluation lowered accuracy for the identical pipeline from 94.57% to 78.41% ± 0.74%, close to the {pm(agg['acc']['mean'], agg['acc']['sd'])} obtained here under the same kind of protocol [@jamaludin2026], and that studies claiming above 90% were consistently associated with undisclosed or leakage-prone protocols. Second, macro-F1 under natural prevalence is intrinsically more punishing than accuracy when the majority class (NV) is roughly 58 times more frequent than the rarest (DF), and independent multi-seed evidence shows macro-F1 varying by a factor of two across folds and seeds while accuracy stays comparatively stable [@dagoglu2025]. Third, none of the higher-accuracy studies in [[T:sota]] reports whether lesions were held out at the lesion level, so they are not shown to be wrong, only not directly comparable to a lesion-level, multi-seed, natural-prevalence estimate.")
    M.p(f"The same logic extends to PAD-UFES-20. A drop of this magnitude is not unusual: the best algorithm of the largest controlled multi-team comparison of dermoscopic classifiers fell from 82.0% balanced accuracy on HAM10000 to 58.8% on BCN20000, with out-of-training categories misclassified as malignant in 47.1% of cases [@combalia2022]; some decrease was found in 81% of 86 externally validated radiologic algorithms and a decrease of at least 0.10 in 24% [@yu2022ext]; and even within-archive metadata groupings produce measurable shifts in dermoscopy [@fogelberg2023]. Against this evidence, the residual appearance-and-label-semantics drop quantified in Section 4.9 is consistent with, not exceptional relative to, the external-validation literature. The 94.8% macro-F1 retention claimed for the same dataset pair by one recent, non-replicated study [@sekaran2026] is the outlier, not the present result, and is listed in [[T:sota]] for completeness, not as a benchmark this study fell short of.")
    M.h(2, '5.9 Practical significance, generalizability and limitations of the evaluation framework')
    M.p("The audit protocol developed here (subtracting a matched null before interpreting a mask-overlap score, evaluating faithfulness and localization as distinct properties with explicit baselines, repeating the pipeline over independent seeds, and decomposing an external drop into population, label-space, prevalence and residual components) does not depend on DenseNet121, Grad-CAM++ or HAM10000. The mathematics of Section 3.8 holds for any post-hoc class-activation-style map scored against a binary reference region; the shuffled, shift and ceiling constructions need only a saliency map and a mask; the perturbation protocol (Section 3.9) needs only a probability-producing classifier and a patch partition of its input, applicable to convolutional and, with a suitable patch definition, transformer classifiers; and the decomposition (Section 3.12) needs only a source and a target label space with a documented mapping. The geometric confound is plausible in other modalities using mask-overlap scores [@saporta2022,arun2021], but this was not tested here. Mathematical generality does not guarantee empirical transfer: Section 4.12 finds that the geometric confound and majority-positive insertion faithfulness replicate on a second architecture while the sign of the area-adjusted excess LSAS and the positivity of excess insertion/deletion gain do not, so generality of the method and of a specific result are separate questions, the first supported by the mathematics and the second only by direct testing.")
    M.p("The framework has boundaries. It requires a reference segmentation for every image scored for localization; where masks come from a segmentation model, the audit is only as reliable as that model, and no null-controlled audit of the mask itself was performed. The permutation test, shift null and faithfulness curves cost more than raw LSAS or a single deletion/insertion number (a few minutes per seed on one consumer GPU here), and the framework has not been evaluated on attention-based or gradient-free explanation methods, where the argument of Section 3.8 may need modification. Finally, it audits explanation reliability as a measurable property; it does not show that a more reliable explanation is one a clinician would find more useful or actionable, which requires a dermatologist reader study. Agreement between saliency maps and dermatologist-identified clinical criteria has been measured [@giavinabianchi2023], but not against a geometry-matched null or jointly with a perturbation-based faithfulness test.")
